<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beiyang Fleet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a>–<a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Pei-yang Fleet, Beiyang Navy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Beiyang Fleet, or Northern Ocean Fleet, was one of four regional navies of late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a>, sponsored by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a> to guard the approaches to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. Begun in 1871 and equipped mostly with warships built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, among them the battleships Dingyuan and Zhenyuan, it was the largest fleet in Asia in the late 1880s. In 1888 it was formally placed under the Navy Yamen, and its budget was cut. Under Ding Ruchang it suffered heavy losses at the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu River</a> in 1894 and was annihilated at Weihaiwei.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beiyang-fleet.jpg" alt="Beiyang Fleet" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beiyang_fleet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The fleet began in 1871, when four ships from the southern provinces were moved north to patrol the northern waters. At first it was the weakest of the four regional navies, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a>, Viceroy of Zhili and a trusted servant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a>, steered most naval funds to it. In 1884 it was second only to the Nanyang Fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, and by 1890 it was the largest. Before 1888 its budget was two million taels; once it came under the Navy Yamen this fell to 1.3 million, and new construction almost stopped.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The fleet guarded the coast from the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu River</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaozhou-bay">Jiaozhou Bay</a> and the approaches to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. Its headquarters was at Tianjin, with the Dagu shipyards, the Tianjin Arsenal and the naval college; Weihaiwei, on the Shandong Peninsula, became a base in 1887; and Lushunkou, built by a French syndicate between 1880 and 1890 for about three million taels, had the only dock able to repair the two battleships. Besides them the fleet had eight cruisers acquired between 1881 and 1889, the coastal battleship Pingyuan, six British gunboats and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torpedo">torpedo</a> boats.</p><p>Officers came mainly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> academy and from the Beiyang naval college founded in 1880, and 35 cadets trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, at Greenwich, Woolwich and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>. Command went to Ding Ruchang, an army officer without naval training. Foreign advisers included Captain William Lang, who resigned after the Flag-hoisting incident, the German gunnery expert Constantin von Hanneken, and the American Philo Norton McGiffin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-french-war">Sino-French War</a> of 1884 to 1885 the fleet kept out of reach of Admiral Amédée Courbet's squadron. On 3 July 1884 Ding withdrew it to Pei-ho, where it stayed almost idle, and the French persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to delay delivery of Dingyuan, Zhenyuan and Jiyuan until after the war. In February 1885 Li recalled two cruisers sent to help the Nanyang Fleet break the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of Formosa, and in the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> the Nanyang Fleet did little to help the Beiyang. Lack of funds left ships unmaintained and training at a standstill; when Dingyuan fired its 10-inch guns at the start of the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu River</a>, its flying bridge collapsed, throwing Ding and the adviser William Ferdinand Tyler with it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Beiyang Fleet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the third phase, from 1885 to 1895, a Navy Board was created and the <b>Beiyang Fleet</b>, with two German-built battleships, was formally established in 1888 under Admiral Ding Ruchang.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
